<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter Abelard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1079–1142</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Pierre le Pallet, Abailard, Abaelardus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter Abelard was a French philosopher, logician, theologian and poet, born at Le Pallet near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, who became a popular lecturer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and around 1115 master of the cathedral school of Notre Dame. His affair with his student Héloïse d'Argenteuil, their secret marriage and his castration by men sent by her uncle Fulbert drove him into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-saint-denis">Abbey of Saint-Denis</a> as a monk. His teaching on the Trinity was condemned at a synod at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> in 1121 and again at the council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a> in 1141, after charges pressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-ii">Pope Innocent II</a> excommunicated him. Peter the Venerable of Cluny took him in, and he died at the priory of St Marcel near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalon-sur-saone">Chalon-sur-Saône</a> in 1142.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-abelard.jpg" alt="Peter Abelard" width="330" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoni Oleszczyński · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piotr_Abelard_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I found the armoury of logical reasoning more to my liking than the other forms of philosophy, I exchanged all other weapons for these, and to the prizes of victory in war I preferred the battle of minds in disputation.&#8221;</p><footer>Historia Calamitatum (The Story of My Misfortunes), chapter 1 &#183; tr. Henry Adams Bellows, 1922 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Historia_Calamitatum" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Our speech was more of love than of the book which lay open before us; our kisses far outnumbered our reasoned words.&#8221;</p><footer>Historia Calamitatum (The Story of My Misfortunes), chapter 6 &#183; tr. Henry Adams Bellows, 1922 &#183; of his lessons with Héloïse &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Historia_Calamitatum" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
